LiveKit Go SDK v2.18.1 (github.com/livekit/server-sdk-go/v2), protocol v1.49.0,
mediatransportutil and psrpc provide the desktop's LiveKit transport.
  Copyright LiveKit contributors
  License: Apache-2.0 - each module's LICENSE is included in the bundle.

The LiveKit transport uses Pion WebRTC and its RTP, RTCP, ICE, DTLS, SRTP,
SCTP and data-channel dependencies, licensed MIT by the Pion community.
Their exact versions and license texts are included in the generated bundle.

The optional built-in VLESS/REALITY transport includes an adapted Xray-core
v26.3.27 REALITY handshake (MPL-2.0, Xray contributors). Its provenance,
license and complete modified source accompany the application under
THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/vendored/internal/reality. The REALITY and uTLS module
licenses are included in the Go module bundle. Xray runs separately on the
server and is not embedded as an executable in the desktop application.

Other Go runtime dependencies include the Go standard library, Wails v3,
adrg/xdg, godbus/dbus, go-ole, golang.org/x/net, golang.org/x/sys,
google.golang.org/protobuf and, on
Windows only, go-toast (Unlicense or MIT), which Wails' notification service
uses to raise toasts. Their
versions and complete license texts are included in the generated bundle.
Wails also contains
attributed runtime code derived from ringqueue, atotto/clipboard and
fyne-io/systray, plus a screen manager inspired by Chromium; the corresponding
licenses, notices and licensed source are preserved. Wails' Windows layer also
retains winc and w32 project copyrights; both upstream license texts are
included.

The separate gul-relay utility uses coder/websocket and quic-go. The generated
bundle includes the union of dependencies of the shipped desktop targets and
this utility; it excludes test-only dependencies and retired transports.

The embedded web frontend includes IBM Plex Sans, IBM Plex Mono and Martian
Mono (SIL Open Font License 1.1), Phosphor Icons, React, React DOM, Scheduler,
Zustand and the Wails JavaScript runtime. The Wails runtime contains attributed
code derived from Nano ID, is-callable and HTMX; their complete licensed source
is preserved separately because production minification removes comments.
Vite's injected modulepreload runtime is covered by its aggregate license file.

The screen-sharing frontend uses livekit-client 2.22.3
(Apache-2.0, LiveKit contributors) and its runtime dependencies. Their versions
and license texts are included by the npm production dependency collector.
LiveKit server 1.13.8 (Apache-2.0) runs separately as a local Docker container;
it is not embedded in the desktop binary.

The portable Windows build is produced with GCC 15.2.0 and MinGW-w64 runtime
v13 (POSIX threads, SEH, UCRT) and statically links the GCC/libstdc++/libgcc,
MinGW-w64 runtime and winpthreads support needed by the executable. GCC's GPLv3
license and Runtime Library Exception, the MinGW-w64 runtime notices, and the
winpthreads license are included under
THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/vendored/third_party/toolchain-runtime. Exact toolchain
provenance is recorded in third_party/toolchain-runtime/VERSION.
